A||||6||saleenfiero||Wheel Painting Help???!??!?!||||5||
Z||000000||saleenfiero||10-20-2000||08:27 PM||||Seeing as I don't have the financial horsepower to invest in a set of 20" American Racing Torq-Thrusts right now, I've decided to paint my stock turbo rims (or whatever they're called) body color - bright red.  What is the best process to do this?  Is there any way to get the cool translucent metallic effect like on the 2000 trans-am anniversary cars?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Oreif||10-20-2000||11:47 PM||||You need to strip the wheels and clean them up. I used Permatex spray-on gasket remover to strip my wheels. Then sand them down with<BR>220, then 420, then 600 grit sandpaper. You want to remove the machine lines out of the face. Polish with Mothers Aluminum polish for a mirror like finish. Then wash the wheel with Duplicolor grease and wax remover. You need to buy a clear urethane paint. (sometimes called "wheel paint") Mix in the "Candy" color of your choice, (Candy paints are translucent) until you get the desired color. Then mask off any portion you don't want painted and spray the rest. The urethane paint is used because of the temp variance of the wheels during driving. I have seen this done with blue and it came out looking really good on a set of 5-star chevy wheels for a Z-24. Looked like it was chrome blue.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Philphine||10-21-2000||05:28 PM||||i'd like to paint or have powder coated a newer and larger style of wheel for my indy. i figure i can do that and still keep my original wheels for later originality purposes. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||10-21-2000||06:34 PM||||Gasket remover works I guess. I used regular paint remover last time I did this. Next time I want to try some of the citrus based stuff. It's slower but it's less toxic.<P>Once they are cleaned you can do all sorts of things to them. Paint, powder, even anodize.<P>Powder coating is pretty expencive but it would definatly work. You might want to look at <A HREF="http://www.eastwoodco.com" TARGET=_blank>Eastwood</A> They have a powder coating kit that's not horribly expencive. You can control powder coverage the same way as paint.<P>Anodize would look nice but it is real expencive. I don't know if you can selectivly anodize areas. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Master_Sushi||10-21-2000||09:00 PM||||Is it necessary to have the tires takes off the rims to strip/paint the wheels?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Oreif||10-21-2000||10:15 PM||||You can leave the tires on, But it's a lot easier with them off. (Less to mask off) Caution some paint strippers will eat rubber. So if you get some at the tire bead you could end up with rim leaks.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Seanh||10-21-2000||10:23 PM||||It would be easier to have them removed, but its not nessacery. I did mine a few months back. I left the tires on, but removed the balancing weights. It cost me $8 a rim to be rebalanced, and I could have had them take the tires off the rims, then remount them once I was done refinishing them, and rebalance them for $40 for the set of 4. Stupid thinking on my part, but it can be done without removing the tires, but you will have to remove the balancing weights. Not sure if you can remount them yourself, my wheels were out of balance to begin with, so I had them rebalanced. I masked off the tire with old shop rags, as well as the gray part in the center of the wheel because it was not bad, I only wanted to shine up the rims. I jacked the car up, removed all wheels, and did them one at a time by putting each one on the rear of the car, and running it in 1st gear. I didnt strip the wheel, I just started out sanding it with <BR>220, 400, 600 grit water proof sandpaper, I diped the paper in water frequently to keep it wet. After all the machine marks were out, I buffed with stainless steel compound, and then with jewlers rough to finish it off. Then after I was happy with it, sprayed the whole wheel with clear coat, then waxed it with car wax.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
